$DIR/main.p:14:2: cannot pass seq to vec.(*Vec).AppendSeq, which keeps it after returning
    seq points into x. x is a local variable of f (declared at main.p:12).
    vec.(*Vec).AppendSeq passes a pointer loaded from *seq to the function value
    (which may call the loop body at vec.p:377) (vec.p:377), which passes
    argument 1 to (*Vec).Push (vec.p:378), which passes x to rt.InitValue
    (vec.p:152), which stores it in memory another argument points to, so seq
    would outlive x.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    If vec.(*Vec).AppendSeq should keep its own copy of x, change it to take the
    value, not a pointer, or an owner it can keep: an owned[int] from new, a
    shared[int], or a library owner such as a vec.Vec.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3003" for a longer explanation)
